Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: mt7530: busy-wait for a PHY register access

This kernel has no high-resolution timers, so the sleep between the polls of
MT7531_PHY_IAC in the indirect PHY accessors is a whole tick, 4 ms at HZ=250,
and a clause-45 access (two transactions) takes 8 ms where the bus needs well
under a tenth of that. The 10 GbE PHYs sit on this bus, and the RTL8261C's
firmware is 4714 register writes: bringing the IQ port up took 38 s on a
W1700K2, at boot before the SSH server starts. Poll with udelay() between
reads, as 0012 does for the NAND.

--- a/drivers/net/dsa/mt7530.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/mt7530.c
@@ -545,8 +545,9 @@
 
 	mt7530_mutex_lock(priv);
 
-	ret = regmap_read_poll_timeout(priv->regmap, MT7531_PHY_IAC, val,
-				       !(val & MT7531_PHY_ACS_ST), 20, 100000);
+	ret = regmap_read_poll_timeout_atomic(priv->regmap, MT7531_PHY_IAC, val,
+					      !(val & MT7531_PHY_ACS_ST), 20,
+					      100000);
 	if (ret < 0) {
 		dev_err(priv->dev, "poll timeout\n");
 		goto out;
@@ -556,8 +557,9 @@
 	      MT7531_MDIO_DEV_ADDR(devad) | regnum;
 	mt7530_mii_write(priv, MT7531_PHY_IAC, reg | MT7531_PHY_ACS_ST);
 
